// A debugger's view of generic structs (bin/run-tests runs it under gdb
// with generic_structs.gdb and under cdb with generic_structs.cdb). Each
// instance is a struct type of its own (spec 3.23, 10.7), named as the
// instance is, Pair(int, bool), at its generic's line, its fields typed
// with its arguments: a Ring(int, 3)'s items an array of 3 int64s, a
// List(int)'s next a pointer to List(int) itself, which both debuggers
// follow, head.next.value in gdb, head.next->value in cdb. Both say
// struct before the name, as for any struct: gdb's whatis and ptype
// `struct Pair(int, bool)`, cdb's dv /t `struct Pair(int, bool) p`, and
// cdb's ?? of a struct shows an array field by its count and first
// element, `items : [3] 0n0`. An alias is the same type under a second
// name, with no debug type of its own: p, declared an Int_Pair, shows as
// a Pair(int, bool).
printf :: (format: *u8, ..) -> s32 #foreign libc;

Pair :: struct (A: Type, B: Type) { first: A; second: B; }
Ring :: struct (T: Type, N: int) { items: [N]T; }
List :: struct (T: Type) { value: T; next: *List(T); }
Int_Pair :: Pair(int, bool);

main :: () {
    p: Int_Pair = .{7, true};
    r: Ring(int, 3);
    for 0..2 { r.items[it] = it * 10; }
    tail: List(int) = .{2, null};
    head: List(int) = .{1, *tail};
    printf("%lld %lld %lld\n", p.first, r.items[2], head.next.value);
}
